  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at height 0.5 × loss over x, z from −2 to 2. It is steeper along x, so overshoot shows there first: without noise, x diverges when learning rate > 2/3.",
    "Each step moves by −learning rate × (true slope + noise). Noise is Gaussian with a spread of 2·√((64−b)/(63·b)) per direction for batch size b, so it is exactly zero at b = 64.",
    "Both arrows are drawn at 2× the length of the step they show: orange is the actual noisy step, grey is −learning rate × true slope. The readout gives the true length of the orange step.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when the loss passes 6, which is the loss at the bowl's steep edge, or the ball leaves the bowl (diverged). The loss readout keeps the true value, and the drawing is capped at the edge.",
    "The noise comes from a fixed sequence. A new run carries on with the next values in that sequence. Reset replays the sequence from the start.",
    "With noise, a step lowers the loss only on average. With small batches, a single step can raise it.",
    "The trail shows the start point plus up to 40 landing points. The ghost shows the last kept run."
  ]
}